WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.710
Good morning, Hank! Good morning, Hank. Good
morning, Hank, good morning, Hank... I wonder

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.430
if I've ever said any three-word phrase more
often in my life.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:09.780
Anyway, it's Monday. I hope you enjoyed Venice,
Florida as much as I enjoyed Venice, Italy;

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.650
I was there because the Indianapolis Museum
of Art, where the Yeti is a curator, commissioned

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.199
the 2011 'American Pavillion of the Venice
Biennale', which is a REALLY fancy deal.

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:23.301
For example, not to brag, I went to a party
with Micheal Stipe, the lead singer of REM,

00:00:23.301 --> 00:00:28.480
who wrote the song 'Stand' which you and I
choreographed a dance to in 1989, which our

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.570
dad videotaped and then blessedly lost.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.640
The artist the IMA commissioned is called
Allora &amp; Calzadilla, and the pavilion is a

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.290
total success - it involves overturned tanks
and Olympic decathletes and gymnasts and an

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:46.780
actual ATM machine that is also an actual
pipe organ! [pipe organ music plays]

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.792
Hank, this is the first time the commission
for the Venice Biennale has gone to a museum

00:00:50.792 --> 00:00:55.380
that isn't on either coast or the Art Institute
of Chicago, and I'm really proud of everybody

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.370
at the Indianapolis Museum of Art for making
it such a huge success. It makes me super

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:06.829
happy to be an Indianapolisian. Or Indianapolisicist.
Or Indianapolicious. Indianapolicious.

00:01:06.829 --> 00:01:09.200
Hey, where are you from? Oh me? I'm Indianapolicious.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.020
In other news, Hank, this is my book! It arrived
today from New York, where my editor and publisher

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.659
Julie Strauss-Gabel sent it to me, you know,
with all of her comments on the pages and

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:18.659
everything.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:21.060
I'm very excited, Hank, because even though
Julie and I have been revising this book for

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:24.789
more than a year, we finally reached the point
where she, like, writes on the manuscript,

00:01:24.789 --> 00:01:28.869
and we can do the fun stuff like fighting
about how to spell the word stormtrooper.

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:32.359
And she can write little notes making fun
of me, like about how in my fictional universe

00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:33.369
every day is Friday.

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:37.029
This is a big problem with my writing, Hank.
I'll often write, like, "On Friday, Augustus

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:41.840
Waters called me. Three days later, on Friday,
we went out to lunch," and Julie's just like

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.490
"Are you even trying? Are you even trying
to figure out what day it should be?" and

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:47.950
I'm like "No! I'm not trying! That's your
job."

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.999
But in fact, Hank, while that's the public's
perception of what an editor does, the truth

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:54.869
is somewhat more complicated, as usual. The
real heart of the editorial process happens

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:58.609
way before we ever start fighting about whether
"stormtroopers" is one word or two, and whether

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:03.539
every day can be Friday; it starts when I
send Julie a draft and then she sends me an

00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:04.539
editorial letter.

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:07.770
And that editorial letter isn't about comma
splices--it includes sentences like, "As we

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.739
crack open Augustus's philosophy and contrast
it with Hazel's more connected sense of living,

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:15.019
I also think there's a lot of room to look
more at the question of the nobility, and

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:20.010
frankly the epic sexiness, of sacrifice/violence
versus the unsung struggle of illness; in

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.870
short: what constitutes martyrdom?"

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.310
As you can see, Hank, that's not about spelling.
By the way, Julie, if you're watching this,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:30.380
I am not changing my spelling of stormtroopers
unless and until George Lucas himself calls

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.540
me and tells me that it's two words. That
said, you're right about everything else,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.849
and, no matter how happy it would make Rebecca
Black, I agree with you that every day should

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:37.849
not be Friday.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:41.490
So, Hank, I think the assumption that editors
exist primarily to, like, fix grammar errors

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.900
is really incorrect. I mean, I could just
read the universe through the Chicago Style

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:49.170
Manual; I don't need Julie for that. But!
I also think it's incorrect when people think

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.019
that the main reason editors exist is to,
like, censor your work, or to somehow make

00:02:53.019 --> 00:02:54.019
it worse.

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:57.430
Without Julie, Paper Towns would be devoted
largely not to Walt Whitman's poem Song of

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.870
Myself but to an incredibly boring history
of the machinations of the United States Postal

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.760
Service. And without Julie, instead of Colin
and Hassan hunting for feral hogs in rural

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.530
Tennessee, there would be this 75-page--and
I'm not making this up--how-to guide about

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.290
how to take a roadkilled raccoon, skin it,
and then tan its hide.

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:18.579
I don't know what I was thinking, Hank, but
back in 2005 I was really interested in hide

00:03:18.579 --> 00:03:19.579
tanning.

00:03:19.579 --> 00:03:23.650
And without Julie and before her my amazing
mentor and first editor Ilene Cooper, nothing

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.069
that anyone likes about Looking for Alaska
would be in that book.

00:03:27.069 --> 00:03:29.909
In short, Hank, while God knows I'd like to
think that writers are more important than

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:34.720
editors, the truth is that we may not be.
I mean, there is a reason that The Great Gatsby

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.530
and The Sun Also Rises were edited by the
same guy.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:43.000
Hank, I'm going to get to work. I'll see you
on Friday. [laughs] I didn't--I didn't even

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:47.029
do that on purpose, I promise. Ohhhh, it's
like I have calendrically specific dementia.

